### ─── §8. Mutations on stress data ───────────────────────────────────────────
### §8a. Update a single document — mark stress_000000 as in-stock, new price
POST http://localhost:1538/update
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "stress",
  "data": {
    "stress_000000": {
      "in_stock": true,
      "price": 399
    }
  }
}

### §8b. Update multiple documents at once
POST http://localhost:1538/update
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "stress",
  "data": {
    "stress_000001": {
      "price": 1099
    },
    "stress_000002": {
      "price": 1199,
      "in_stock": false
    }
  }
}

### §8c. Delete a single document
POST http://localhost:1538/delete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "stress",
  "keys": "stress_099999"
}

### §8d. Delete multiple documents
POST http://localhost:1538/delete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "stress",
  "keys": [
    "stress_099998",
    "stress_099997",
    "stress_099996"
  ]
}

### §8e. Bulk delete with count — remove at most 233 gaming laptops (oldest first)
### `order` decides which matches go first when `count` caps the delete. Matches are
### sorted by `_seq` before the cap, so the delete is deterministic. Default is "asc"
### (oldest first, lowest `_seq`); pass "desc" to remove the newest first.
POST http://localhost:1538/delete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "stress",
  "where": {
    "tags": {
      "$contains": "gaming"
    }
  },
  "count": 233,
  "order": "asc"
}

### §8e-2. Bulk delete with count — remove the 233 NEWEST gaming laptops
POST http://localhost:1538/delete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "stress",
  "where": {
    "tags": {
      "$contains": "gaming"
    }
  },
  "count": 233,
  "order": "desc"
}

### §8e-3. Count-only delete — remove the 500 OLDEST documents (no `where`)
### With no `keys`/`where`/`drop`, only a `count`, MoltenDB removes the oldest (default)
### or newest `n` documents by `_seq`. It reads the ordered `_seq` index directly (like an
### unsorted GET) — no scan, no predicate, no decode — so it's the cheapest prune primitive.
### `count` is REQUIRED here (no default) so a tiny payload can never silently wipe data.
POST http://localhost:1538/delete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "stress",
  "count": 500
}

### §8e-4. Count-only delete — remove the 500 NEWEST documents (order=desc)
POST http://localhost:1538/delete
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "stress",
  "count": 500,
  "order": "desc"
}

### §8f. Verify deletions — these should return empty results
POST http://localhost:1538/get
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "stress",
  "keys": [
    "stress_099999",
    "stress_099998",
    "stress_099997",
    "stress_099996"
  ]
}

### Verify count: how many docs match price=3499 + tags contains gaming?
POST http://localhost:1538/get
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "collection": "stress",
  "fields": [
    "_key"
  ],
  "where": {
    "price": {
      "$eq": 3499
    },
    "tags": {
      "$ct": "gaming"
    }
  }
}

